The meta-schema is deliberately self-contained. No inheritance from gist types — no "sub Intention", no reliance on gist's relations. This lets Lingo work with any ontology (gist-based vibe-machine ontologies today, a SIM-derived ontology tomorrow) without special-casing.

Three decisions drive the shape:

1. Source-agnostic. No references to gist, vibe-machine, or any other package. An ontology that has no upper ontology at all can still run Lingo. Specializing ChangeIntent sub Intention on gist-based sources is a future enhancement, not part of v1.

2. Collision-safe names. gist already defines relation `precedes` (roles predecessor / successor). Reusing that name collides. We use unique names that do not appear in gist, vibemachine, or the self-describing schema: `changeSequence` (roles earlier / later) for the intent-order chain, `changeConcept` (roles intent / concept) for intent grouping.

3. Bundling via Swift string constant. The Ontology plugin target's copy phase only ships Resources/Packages. The fragment is embedded directly as a Swift constant in OneApps/Ontology/Sandbox/ChangeIntentSchema.swift. It ships because it's code. Zero bundling concerns, small payload (<1KB), and humans can still review it as TQL text.

targetKind enum (string values — the meta-schema treats targetKind as a string attribute, enumeration is a caller-side convention documented here):
  - "entity"      — entity type added, removed, or modified
  - "attribute"   — attribute type added, removed, or modified
  - "relation"    — relation type added, removed, or modified
  - "role"        — role on a relation added or removed; targetLabel format: "<relation>.<role>"
  - "owns"        — owns clause added or removed; targetLabel format: "<owner>.<attribute>"
  - "sub"         — parent-type declaration added, removed, or changed; targetLabel format: "<type>.sub"
  - "function"    — function added, removed, or body changed; targetLabel = function name (simple identifier, no delimiter)

Sandbox metadata: SandboxMeta is part of this same fragment because every sandbox needs durable metadata (sandboxName, sourceName, createdAt, creatorVersion) for SandboxManager.list() and .reopen() to honestly populate SandboxHandle.

Schema shape (final):

  define
    entity ChangeIntent,
      owns action,
      owns targetLabel,
      owns targetKind,
      owns renameFrom @card(0..1),
      owns motivation @card(0..1),
      owns typeqlStatement,
      owns timestamp;

    entity SandboxMeta,
      owns sandboxName,
      owns sourceName,
      owns createdAt,
      owns creatorVersion;

    relation changeSequence,
      relates earlier,
      relates later;

    relation changeConcept,
      relates intent,
      relates concept;

    ChangeIntent plays changeSequence:earlier, plays changeSequence:later;
    ChangeIntent plays changeConcept:intent;

    attribute action, value string;
    attribute targetLabel, value string;
    attribute targetKind, value string;
    attribute renameFrom, value string;
    attribute motivation, value string;
    attribute typeqlStatement, value string;
    attribute timestamp, value datetime;
    attribute sandboxName, value string;
    attribute sourceName, value string;
    attribute createdAt, value datetime;
    attribute creatorVersion, value string;

No schema-level enforcement of targetKind values; the fragment stays simple. Callers (ChangeIntentWriter, DriftDetector) use the documented enum.

