The full Phase 2 synthesis workflow. Composes Generator + Judge + (Migrator | direct) + Interrupt (user confirmation) + Committer using Swarm orchestration primitives: Sequential, RepeatWhile (bounded retries), Branch (additive vs breaking), Guard (escalate after max retries), Interrupt (user confirmation gate).

File: OneApps/Ontology/Agent/Synthesis/SynthesisWorkflow.swift

```swift
import Foundation
import SwarmAgent

enum SynthesisWorkflow {
  @MainActor
  static func make(viewModel: OntologyViewModel) -> any OrchestrationStep {
    let generator = SchemaGeneratorAgent.make(viewModel: viewModel)
    let judge = JudgeStep.make(viewModel: viewModel)
    let migrator = MigratorAgent.make(viewModel: viewModel)
    let committer = CommitterStep(viewModel: viewModel)

    return Sequential {
      // 1. Generator/Judge loop with bounded retries.
      //    Loop continues while the latest verdict is "reject" AND retries are below max.
      RepeatWhile(
        condition: { result in shouldRetryGenerator(result) },
        maxIterations: 3
      ) {
        Sequential {
          generator
          judge
        }
      }

      // 2. After the loop: if still rejected (max retries hit) or verdict is needsClarification,
      //    escalate by failing the Sequential. The parent workflow (the per-turn workflow,
      //    composed elsewhere as ELICITATION → SYNTHESIS) handles the escalation by routing
      //    back to the Interviewer next turn.
      Guard(
        name: "JudgeAccepted",
        check: { result in result.metadata["judgeVerdict"] == .string("accept") },
        message: "Judge did not accept — escalating to clarification"
      )

      // 3. Branch on additive vs breaking.
      //    If breaking: run Migrator. Migrator may produce ambiguities, in which case the
      //    Migrator's output is itself a clarification request — handled via a second Guard.
      Branch(
        condition: { result in result.metadata["isBreaking"] == .bool(true) },
        ifTrue: Sequential {
          migrator
          Guard(
            name: "MigrationUnambiguous",
            check: { result in !hasAmbiguities(result) },
            message: "Migrator returned ambiguities — escalating to clarification"
          )
        },
        ifFalse: nil  // additive path: skip Migrator
      )

      // 4. User confirmation gate.
      //    Workflow pauses until external action (UI confirmation event) resumes.
      Interrupt(reason: .awaitingUserConfirmation)

      // 5. Commit.
      committer
    }
  }

  // Helpers (composed result inspection):

  private static func shouldRetryGenerator(_ result: AgentResult) -> Bool {
    guard let verdict = result.metadata["judgeVerdict"]?.stringValue else { return false }
    return verdict == "reject"
  }

  private static func hasAmbiguities(_ result: AgentResult) -> Bool {
    guard let output = result.output.data(using: .utf8),
          let plan = try? JSONDecoder().decode(MigrationPlan.self, from: output) else {
      return false
    }
    return !plan.ambiguities.isEmpty
  }
}
```

Workflow shape (textual):

```
Sequential {
  RepeatWhile (verdict == reject, max=3) {
    Sequential { Generator → Judge }
  }
  Guard "JudgeAccepted"
  Branch (isBreaking) {
    ifTrue: Sequential { Migrator → Guard "MigrationUnambiguous" }
    ifFalse: skip
  }
  Interrupt (awaiting user confirmation)
  Committer
}
```

Critical design choices:

1. RepeatWhile body is `Sequential { Generator → Judge }`. Each iteration = one full propose-and-judge cycle. Reject reason from Judge feeds back to Generator on the next iteration via context (verify exact mechanism in 38.4.1; AgentContext['retryFeedback'] is the likely shape).

2. Guard "JudgeAccepted" after the loop catches the case where max iterations exhausted with no acceptance. Failing the Guard escalates: parent per-turn workflow catches this and routes back to the Interviewer.

3. Branch on isBreaking. Additive path skips Migrator entirely (most user actions are additive — meaningful optimization).

4. Migrator can itself escalate via ambiguities. That's why Guard "MigrationUnambiguous" follows the Migrator inside the Branch.

5. Interrupt before Committer. The user must confirm via the UI before any change reaches the live sandbox. The chat surface provides the confirmation affordance; resuming the workflow happens when the user accepts. (The Interrupt's resume mechanism is Swarm-defined; verify in 38.4.1 audit and document the exact resume path here once known.)

6. Composition with the parent per-turn workflow: SynthesisWorkflow is composed downstream of ElicitationStep. Whether they live in one Workflow or two depends on whether the user wants to re-elicit on every turn or only run synthesis when ELICITATION indicates the user is ready. Defer that wiring decision to the per-turn workflow assembly (a separate, smaller piece — could be a follow-up task).

