## Hierarchy Rules
Valid parent-child relationships:
- Epic → Feature
- Epic → Task
- Feature → Task

A Task should only have ONE structural parent (either Epic OR Feature, not both).

New view-model state on OntologyViewModel:
- pinnedLivePackagesBySandbox: [String: PinnedLivePackages] — keyed by sandbox.name.
- struct PinnedLivePackages { let entities: [ParsedEntity]; let relations: [ParsedRelation]; let attributes: [ParsedAttribute] } — flat aggregation over installed packages for that sandbox.

Refresh flow:
@MainActor
func refreshPinnedLivePackages(for sandbox: SandboxHandle) async {
  let installed = try await sandboxInstalledPackagesService.installedPackages(in: sandbox)
  var entities: [ParsedEntity] = []; var relations: [ParsedRelation] = []; var attributes: [ParsedAttribute] = []
  for pkg in installed {
    let parsed = try await packageVersionResolver.resolve(packageName: pkg.packageName, version: pkg.version)
    entities.append(contentsOf: parsed.entities); /* ... */
  }
  pinnedLivePackagesBySandbox[sandbox.name] = PinnedLivePackages(entities, relations, attributes)
}

Triggers:
- Active sandbox change → refresh for new sandbox.
- apply_schema_delta success → invalidate + refresh for the affected sandbox.
- Manual user refresh (a button on the explorer banner or sidebar header).

Lingo Explorer's tree assembly: visibleLingoExplorerRows now reads from pinnedLivePackagesBySandbox[activeSandbox.name] for the live sections, falling back to OntologyViewModel.parsedEntities (V1 source) when no entry exists. The fallback is the no-sandbox state handled in T4.4.

Cross-reference resolution: detail-pane links resolve against the pinned live data, not PackageManager.

Out of scope: changing the live entity browser. It continues to read OntologyViewModel.parsedEntities directly. Only Lingo Explorer's sidebar re-sources.

Likely shape (refined by audit findings):

protocol ExplorerHostable: AnyObject {
  associatedtype Selection: Hashable
  var visibleRows: [ExplorerRow<Self>] { get }
  var expandedNodes: Set<String> { get set }
  var selection: Selection? { get set }
  var searchText: String { get set }
  var orderedDomains: [String] { get }
  func selectRow(_ row: ExplorerRow<Self>)
  func toggleExpansion(_ row: ExplorerRow<Self>)
}

ExplorerRow becomes generic over the host (or carries a custom node-kind enum so each explorer can extend with proposal-specific cases without polluting the shared base).

Reusable view: ExplorerHostView<H: ExplorerHostable> — the 3-pane HSplitView shell, parameterized by host. Subviews (sidebar tree, row, detail switch, inspector) accept the host as a generic parameter or a closure.

Proposal-specific extension: LingoExplorerHost adds banner, diff badges, edit chrome via separate views/closures injected into ExplorerHostView. The shared base never sees 'proposal' as a concept.

Decision: protocol vs single concrete type. Lean protocol to keep test scaffolding light and let the live and Lingo hosts have different storage (OntologyViewModel vs a thin wrapper).

Tests: a fixture host implementing the protocol can render in a snapshot test, proving the shared view layer doesn't depend on either concrete explorer.
